  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 200 AM CDT Sat Aug 8 2026\n\nSunday...Could be an interesting day but plenty of uncertainty, \nwith morning elevated precip and debris clouds possibly tempering \nthe heat up potential, as well as the ongoing short wave passage \nthat day and the sfc front trying to retreat back to the north as a \nwarm front. The big question is where the LLVL thermal axis lays out \nfor LLVL convergence to act on for renewed convection initiation to \ntake off Sunday afternoon. With current effective shear progs of 35-\n45 KTs helped in part by strengthening mid level jet flow, and \npotential MUCAPEs of 2-3K J/kg, there looks to be a combo of support \nfor severe storms and damaging winds. But again where this axis of \ndevelopment occurs is still uncertain. Several ensembles suggest \nthis axis could be right acrs the local area say from north central \nIA into north central IN Sunday evening, while several other models \nlay it out for most of the action to occur just off to the north and \nnortheast acrs WI. Depending on what occurs late Sat night into \nSunday morning will determine what occurs Sunday afternoon and \nevening(boundary and outflow lay-out), but will have to take into \naccount that at least a portion of the local CWA may be in line for \ntrouble and potentially nasty storms.\n\nThe same thinking about boundaries and cloud debris will have to be \ntaken into account if ambient temps can warm up enough to support \nheat headlines. Right now we are advertising high temps in the upper \n80s to around 90 with at least mid 70 DPTs making for heat index \nreadings in the low 100s along and south of I-80. With the \nuncertainty, will hold off any heat headlines for now.\n\nMonday through Friday...With the flattening ridge transition, the \nlocal area may be under the battle zone of the LLVL thermal gradient \nand upper level short wave propagation path for almost daily chance \nof showers and storms for much of next week. If not for cloud debris \nand boundary placement, Monday and Tuesday may be hot enough for \nheat headlines as well at least for portions of the local area(most \nlikley the south half). A deep summer MCS-track for much of the week \nfrom the central plains over to the southern GRT LKS and OH RVR \nValley may add up to quite a bit of rainfall by next Thursday or \nFriday.",
